1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,721
She looked him in the eyes,

2
00:00:01,721 --> 00:00:02,869
in a court of law,

3
00:00:02,869 --> 00:00:05,546
and said: that is the man who attacked me.

4
00:00:05,546 --> 00:00:07,714
She had never been more certain of

5
00:00:07,714 --> 00:00:09,053
anything in her life.

6
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:12,049
And she was completely,
catastrophically wrong.

7
00:00:12,049 --> 00:00:14,536
What she and that innocent man chose to

8
00:00:14,536 --> 00:00:17,086
do next is almost harder to believe than

9
00:00:17,086 --> 00:00:18,297
the mistake itself.

10
00:00:18,647 --> 00:00:20,804
This is the story of how the most

11
00:00:20,804 --> 00:00:23,027
careful, most confident eyewitness

12
00:00:23,027 --> 00:00:25,707
imaginable sent an innocent man to prison

13
00:00:25,707 --> 00:00:27,275
for more than a decade —

14
00:00:27,275 --> 00:00:29,628
how the real culprit was not a liar,

15
00:00:29,628 --> 00:00:31,263
but human memory itself —

16
00:00:31,263 --> 00:00:33,420
and how the two of them ended up,

17
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:35,969
years later, standing on the same side.

18
00:00:36,319 --> 00:00:37,701
In the summer of 1984,

19
00:00:37,701 --> 00:00:39,710
in Burlington, North Carolina, a

20
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:42,285
twenty-two-year-old college student named

21
00:00:42,285 --> 00:00:44,860
Jennifer Thompson was attacked in her own

22
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:46,367
apartment at knifepoint.

23
00:00:46,367 --> 00:00:48,565
In the middle of the worst night of

24
00:00:48,565 --> 00:00:51,014
her life, she made a decision that most

25
00:00:51,014 --> 00:00:52,207
people never could.

26
00:00:52,207 --> 00:00:54,845
She forced herself to study her attacker —

27
00:00:54,845 --> 00:00:57,168
his face, his voice, every detail she

28
00:00:57,168 --> 00:00:58,236
could hold onto —

29
00:00:58,236 --> 00:00:59,555
so that if she lived,

30
00:00:59,555 --> 00:01:01,878
she could make certain he was caught.

31
00:01:01,878 --> 00:01:02,694
She did live.

32
00:01:02,694 --> 00:01:05,144
And she was determined to get it right.

33
00:01:05,144 --> 00:01:07,342
That determination matters, because

34
00:01:07,342 --> 00:01:09,979
everything that went wrong here went wrong

35
00:01:09,979 --> 00:01:10,921
in spite of it.

36
00:01:10,921 --> 00:01:12,303
She was, in every way,

37
00:01:12,303 --> 00:01:14,563
the witness a prosecutor dreams of —

38
00:01:14,563 --> 00:01:16,071
sober, deliberate, sure.

39
00:01:16,071 --> 00:01:18,897
And that is exactly what
should frighten you.

40
00:01:18,897 --> 00:01:21,220
If the system could get it this wrong

41
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:22,978
with a witness this careful,

42
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:24,988
it can get it wrong with anyone.

43
00:01:25,338 --> 00:01:28,247
Police built a composite sketch from her

44
00:01:28,247 --> 00:01:29,120
description.

45
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:30,938
A tip brought in a name —

46
00:01:30,938 --> 00:01:32,029
Ronald Cotton —

47
00:01:32,029 --> 00:01:33,265
and a photograph.

48
00:01:33,265 --> 00:01:35,810
Detectives laid out a lineup of six

49
00:01:35,810 --> 00:01:38,646
photos, and Thompson studied them hard.

50
00:01:38,646 --> 00:01:40,319
She narrowed it to two.

51
00:01:40,319 --> 00:01:42,573
"It's between these," she said,

52
00:01:42,573 --> 00:01:45,919
before finally settling
on one: Ronald Cotton.

53
00:01:45,919 --> 00:01:48,609
Days later, they brought her in for a

54
00:01:48,609 --> 00:01:49,846
physical lineup —

55
00:01:49,846 --> 00:01:51,955
real men, standing in a row —

56
00:01:51,955 --> 00:01:53,991
and again, she chose Cotton.

57
00:01:54,341 --> 00:01:55,746
Now hold on to one detail,

58
00:01:55,746 --> 00:01:58,178
because it is the hinge
of this entire story.

59
00:01:58,178 --> 00:02:00,177
Ronald Cotton was the only person who

60
00:02:00,177 --> 00:02:02,393
appeared in both the photo lineup and the

61
00:02:02,393 --> 00:02:03,093
live lineup.

62
00:02:03,093 --> 00:02:04,984
By the time she saw him standing in

63
00:02:04,984 --> 00:02:07,145
front of her, she had already spent time

64
00:02:07,145 --> 00:02:08,442
studying his photograph.

65
00:02:08,442 --> 00:02:10,442
She may not have been recognizing her

66
00:02:10,442 --> 00:02:11,306
attacker at all.

67
00:02:11,306 --> 00:02:13,522
She may have been recognizing the picture

68
00:02:13,522 --> 00:02:14,821
the police had shown her.

69
00:02:15,171 --> 00:02:16,646
And when she chose him,

70
00:02:16,646 --> 00:02:19,660
the detectives told her
she'd done a great job.

71
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:22,033
Later, when she asked whether she had

72
00:02:22,033 --> 00:02:24,598
picked the right man, they told her yes.

73
00:02:24,598 --> 00:02:27,163
Each reassurance did something quiet and

74
00:02:27,163 --> 00:02:27,863
permanent.

75
00:02:27,863 --> 00:02:30,492
It took a woman who had hesitated between

76
00:02:30,492 --> 00:02:32,929
two photographs and slowly, invisibly,

77
00:02:32,929 --> 00:02:35,046
turned her into a witness who was

78
00:02:35,046 --> 00:02:36,841
absolutely, unshakably sure.

79
00:02:36,841 --> 00:02:39,022
None of this was a trick played on

80
00:02:39,022 --> 00:02:40,048
a foolish woman.

81
00:02:40,048 --> 00:02:42,356
It is how memory works in all of us.

82
00:02:42,356 --> 00:02:44,793
A face glimpsed in terror is not filed

83
00:02:44,793 --> 00:02:47,166
away like a photograph; it is a rough

84
00:02:47,166 --> 00:02:47,866
sketch —

85
00:02:47,866 --> 00:02:50,111
and every time we are asked to look

86
00:02:50,111 --> 00:02:52,419
again, the mind quietly fills in the

87
00:02:52,419 --> 00:02:55,369
blanks, often with
whatever it was just shown.

88
00:02:55,369 --> 00:02:57,678
Add a confident "you did great" from

89
00:02:57,678 --> 00:03:00,051
someone in authority, and a tentative

90
00:03:00,051 --> 00:03:02,488
guess can harden into a certainty that

91
00:03:02,488 --> 00:03:05,502
feels, from the inside,
exactly like the truth.

92
00:03:05,502 --> 00:03:07,490
That is the quiet horror of it.

93
00:03:07,490 --> 00:03:09,478
By the time she took the stand,

94
00:03:09,478 --> 00:03:11,337
she was not guessing at all —

95
00:03:11,337 --> 00:03:13,967
she knew, the way you know your own name.

96
00:03:13,967 --> 00:03:16,468
The mistake had been sealed long before

97
00:03:16,468 --> 00:03:18,263
the trial, in a lineup room,

98
00:03:18,263 --> 00:03:20,828
by people who believed they were helping

99
00:03:20,828 --> 00:03:21,673
her get it right.

100
00:03:22,023 --> 00:03:23,531
By the time of the trial,

101
00:03:23,531 --> 00:03:25,039
that certainty was total.

102
00:03:25,039 --> 00:03:27,029
Jennifer Thompson took the stand,

103
00:03:27,029 --> 00:03:29,019
looked directly at Ronald Cotton,

104
00:03:29,019 --> 00:03:31,130
and told the jury: that is the man.

105
00:03:31,130 --> 00:03:33,603
There is a special, overwhelming power in

106
00:03:33,603 --> 00:03:35,352
a victim who is that certain.

107
00:03:35,352 --> 00:03:37,765
In 1985, it was enough to convict Ronald

108
00:03:37,765 --> 00:03:39,936
Cotton and to send him to prison for

109
00:03:39,936 --> 00:03:41,203
the rest of his life.

110
00:03:41,553 --> 00:03:43,998
Cotton said, from the very first day,

111
00:03:43,998 --> 00:03:45,848
that they had the wrong man.

112
00:03:45,848 --> 00:03:48,425
But no one had a reason to believe him.

113
00:03:48,425 --> 00:03:50,275
He had the word of a man who

114
00:03:50,275 --> 00:03:52,588
claimed to be innocent; she had the

115
00:03:52,588 --> 00:03:55,165
certainty of the woman who had survived

116
00:03:55,165 --> 00:03:55,865
the crime.

117
00:03:55,865 --> 00:03:58,177
In a courtroom, certainty wins that

118
00:03:58,177 --> 00:04:00,358
contest almost every single time.

119
00:04:00,358 --> 00:04:02,869
His conviction was later overturned on

120
00:04:02,869 --> 00:04:03,569
appeal —

121
00:04:03,569 --> 00:04:05,419
and then, at a second trial,

122
00:04:05,419 --> 00:04:07,533
he was convicted all over again,

123
00:04:07,533 --> 00:04:10,044
this time for both attacks that night.

124
00:04:10,044 --> 00:04:12,489
By now he had spent years fighting to

125
00:04:12,489 --> 00:04:14,405
be heard, from inside a cell,

126
00:04:14,405 --> 00:04:17,114
against the one thing a jury trusts most:

127
00:04:17,114 --> 00:04:18,700
a victim's certain word.

128
00:04:18,700 --> 00:04:21,211
He was losing, and running out of ways

129
00:04:21,211 --> 00:04:22,202
to be believed.

130
00:04:22,202 --> 00:04:24,911
His first conviction had been overturned,

131
00:04:24,911 --> 00:04:27,356
only for a second jury to convict him

132
00:04:27,356 --> 00:04:28,413
all over again —

133
00:04:28,413 --> 00:04:30,924
this time for both attacks that night.

134
00:04:30,924 --> 00:04:32,973
He was a man telling the truth,

135
00:04:32,973 --> 00:04:35,285
in a room full of people with every

136
00:04:35,285 --> 00:04:37,862
reason, and every right, to believe the

137
00:04:37,862 --> 00:04:39,845
woman across from him instead.

138
00:04:39,845 --> 00:04:42,343
Innocence, it turned out, was not enough.

139
00:04:42,693 --> 00:04:45,264
But inside the prison, something happened

140
00:04:45,264 --> 00:04:48,085
that stories like this
almost never give you.

141
00:04:48,085 --> 00:04:50,593
Ronald Cotton crossed paths with another

142
00:04:50,593 --> 00:04:51,293
inmate —

143
00:04:51,293 --> 00:04:52,798
a man named Bobby Poole,

144
00:04:52,798 --> 00:04:55,118
locked up for other sexual assaults —

145
00:04:55,118 --> 00:04:57,437
and the resemblance stopped him cold.

146
00:04:57,437 --> 00:04:58,817
Poole looked like him.

147
00:04:58,817 --> 00:05:00,196
So much like him that,

148
00:05:00,196 --> 00:05:03,080
it was said, even guards
confused the two men.

149
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:05,525
And then Cotton learned something more:

150
00:05:05,525 --> 00:05:07,908
Poole had bragged to a fellow prisoner

151
00:05:07,908 --> 00:05:10,353
that Ronald Cotton was doing time for a

152
00:05:10,353 --> 00:05:12,600
crime that Poole himself had committed.

153
00:05:12,950 --> 00:05:15,316
Cotton fought to get that in front of

154
00:05:15,316 --> 00:05:17,427
a court, and at his second trial,

155
00:05:17,427 --> 00:05:20,049
the man he believed was the real attacker

156
00:05:20,049 --> 00:05:22,608
was actually brought into the courtroom,

157
00:05:22,608 --> 00:05:25,294
where Jennifer Thompson could see him with

158
00:05:25,294 --> 00:05:26,125
her own eyes.

159
00:05:26,125 --> 00:05:27,852
She looked at Bobby Poole —

160
00:05:27,852 --> 00:05:30,219
the man who had truly assaulted her —

161
00:05:30,219 --> 00:05:31,306
and felt nothing.

162
00:05:31,306 --> 00:05:32,010
A stranger.

163
00:05:32,010 --> 00:05:34,696
The real perpetrator stood only a few feet

164
00:05:34,696 --> 00:05:36,998
away, and her memory did not so much

165
00:05:36,998 --> 00:05:37,702
as flicker.

166
00:05:37,702 --> 00:05:39,940
The court let the conviction stand.

167
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:42,115
Ronald Cotton went back to prison.

168
00:05:42,115 --> 00:05:44,801
And the question the whole case now turned

169
00:05:44,801 --> 00:05:47,423
on was terrible in its simplicity: if the

170
00:05:47,423 --> 00:05:49,982
witness could look directly at the truth

171
00:05:49,982 --> 00:05:52,604
and not recognize it, what on earth would

172
00:05:52,604 --> 00:05:53,883
it take to prove it?

173
00:05:53,883 --> 00:05:56,506
Think about how complete that failure is.

174
00:05:56,506 --> 00:05:58,744
The one person with every reason to

175
00:05:58,744 --> 00:06:00,407
remember this man's face —

176
00:06:00,407 --> 00:06:02,326
who had studied it on purpose,

177
00:06:02,326 --> 00:06:03,733
to survive the night —

178
00:06:03,733 --> 00:06:05,716
now sat a few feet from him and

179
00:06:05,716 --> 00:06:06,995
felt nothing at all.

180
00:06:06,995 --> 00:06:09,489
If a memory could fail that completely,

181
00:06:09,489 --> 00:06:11,983
then certainty on a witness stand could

182
00:06:11,983 --> 00:06:14,158
never again be trusted on its own.

183
00:06:14,508 --> 00:06:16,399
It would take a science that,

184
00:06:16,399 --> 00:06:18,355
in 1984, had barely been born.

185
00:06:18,355 --> 00:06:21,028
Ronald Cotton served more than ten years.

186
00:06:21,028 --> 00:06:23,506
And across those years, a new tool was

187
00:06:23,506 --> 00:06:25,723
quietly maturing in laboratories —

188
00:06:25,723 --> 00:06:28,266
the ability to read a person's identity

189
00:06:28,266 --> 00:06:29,374
out of their DNA.

190
00:06:29,374 --> 00:06:31,852
Cotton pushed for it, again and again.

191
00:06:31,852 --> 00:06:34,395
The physical evidence from the case had

192
00:06:34,395 --> 00:06:37,133
been preserved, and in 1995 it was finally

193
00:06:37,133 --> 00:06:39,676
sent for testing to a lab in Burlington

194
00:06:39,676 --> 00:06:40,654
called LabCorp.

195
00:06:41,004 --> 00:06:43,214
The result did two things at once.

196
00:06:43,214 --> 00:06:45,620
It excluded Ronald Cotton completely.

197
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:47,440
And it matched Bobby Poole —

198
00:06:47,440 --> 00:06:50,106
the man Thompson's memory had sworn was a

199
00:06:50,106 --> 00:06:51,081
total stranger.

200
00:06:51,431 --> 00:06:53,017
Sit with what that means,

201
00:06:53,017 --> 00:06:54,919
because it is the whole point.

202
00:06:54,919 --> 00:06:57,139
The certainty that had convicted an

203
00:06:57,139 --> 00:06:58,852
innocent man was not a lie.

204
00:06:58,852 --> 00:07:00,944
It was something more unsettling.

205
00:07:00,944 --> 00:07:01,832
It was honest.

206
00:07:01,832 --> 00:07:04,179
Jennifer Thompson had been completely

207
00:07:04,179 --> 00:07:06,082
sincere and completely wrong —

208
00:07:06,082 --> 00:07:08,746
because memory is not a recording you play

209
00:07:08,746 --> 00:07:09,697
back untouched.

210
00:07:09,697 --> 00:07:12,107
It is a story your mind rebuilds every

211
00:07:12,107 --> 00:07:13,502
time you reach for it,

212
00:07:13,502 --> 00:07:15,595
and it can be quietly edited by a

213
00:07:15,595 --> 00:07:17,561
photograph you were shown, or a

214
00:07:17,561 --> 00:07:20,162
detective's kind reassurance, without you

215
00:07:20,162 --> 00:07:21,684
ever feeling the change.

216
00:07:21,684 --> 00:07:24,031
That is what makes an error like this

217
00:07:24,031 --> 00:07:24,855
so dangerous.

218
00:07:24,855 --> 00:07:26,250
A liar can be caught —

219
00:07:26,250 --> 00:07:28,851
in a nervous glance, a story that doesn't

220
00:07:28,851 --> 00:07:29,739
hold together.

221
00:07:29,739 --> 00:07:32,276
But an honest witness who is certain and

222
00:07:32,276 --> 00:07:34,115
wrong carries no tell at all.

223
00:07:34,115 --> 00:07:36,208
Jennifer Thompson's sincerity was

224
00:07:36,208 --> 00:07:38,872
completely real, and it was precisely that

225
00:07:38,872 --> 00:07:41,535
sincerity that made her so convincing to a

226
00:07:41,535 --> 00:07:44,326
jury, and so devastating
to an innocent man.

227
00:07:44,676 --> 00:07:46,972
On June 30th, 1995, after more than a

228
00:07:46,972 --> 00:07:49,267
decade behind bars for a crime he did

229
00:07:49,267 --> 00:07:52,183
not commit, Ronald Cotton
walked out of prison.

230
00:07:52,183 --> 00:07:54,603
He was pardoned, and the state paid him

231
00:07:54,603 --> 00:07:56,774
a small sum for what it had taken —

232
00:07:56,774 --> 00:07:59,318
a little over a hundred thousand dollars,

233
00:07:59,318 --> 00:08:01,303
set against eleven stolen years,

234
00:08:01,303 --> 00:08:03,165
as if a decade of a man's life

235
00:08:03,165 --> 00:08:05,336
could be balanced out with a check.

236
00:08:05,336 --> 00:08:08,314
Bobby Poole later pleaded
guilty to the attacks.

237
00:08:08,664 --> 00:08:10,987
But the truth arrived carrying a cruelty

238
00:08:10,987 --> 00:08:11,687
of its own.

239
00:08:11,687 --> 00:08:14,069
Because now Jennifer Thompson had to live

240
00:08:14,069 --> 00:08:16,334
with the knowledge that her certainty —

241
00:08:16,334 --> 00:08:18,135
careful, well-meant, absolute —

242
00:08:18,135 --> 00:08:20,458
had cost an innocent man eleven years of

243
00:08:20,458 --> 00:08:21,158
his life.

244
00:08:21,158 --> 00:08:23,423
And the question that hung over both of

245
00:08:23,423 --> 00:08:25,805
them was whether Ronald Cotton could ever

246
00:08:25,805 --> 00:08:27,890
forgive the woman who had put him there.

247
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:29,548
Here is his answer.

248
00:08:29,548 --> 00:08:31,820
Ronald Cotton wanted to meet her.

249
00:08:31,820 --> 00:08:32,852
But he waited —

250
00:08:32,852 --> 00:08:34,091
nearly two years —

251
00:08:34,091 --> 00:08:35,468
until she was ready.

252
00:08:35,468 --> 00:08:38,084
And in 1997, in a small church office,

253
00:08:38,084 --> 00:08:40,907
Jennifer Thompson finally sat down across

254
00:08:40,907 --> 00:08:43,385
from the man she had sent to prison.

255
00:08:43,385 --> 00:08:44,487
She was shaking.

256
00:08:44,487 --> 00:08:46,896
She told him that even if she spent

257
00:08:46,896 --> 00:08:49,030
every remaining day of her life

258
00:08:49,030 --> 00:08:51,646
apologizing, it would never be enough.

259
00:08:51,996 --> 00:08:54,581
Ronald Cotton told her that he had already

260
00:08:54,581 --> 00:08:55,442
forgiven her —

261
00:08:55,442 --> 00:08:56,304
years earlier.

262
00:08:56,304 --> 00:08:57,657
That he was not angry.

263
00:08:57,657 --> 00:08:59,688
That he did not want her to carry

264
00:08:59,688 --> 00:09:00,388
it anymore.

265
00:09:00,388 --> 00:09:02,665
He wanted them both to be free of it.

266
00:09:02,665 --> 00:09:04,696
There was no camera in that room,

267
00:09:04,696 --> 00:09:06,234
no audience, no lawsuit —

268
00:09:06,234 --> 00:09:08,326
just two people broken by the same

269
00:09:08,326 --> 00:09:10,726
mistake, deciding not to let it own the

270
00:09:10,726 --> 00:09:11,957
rest of their lives.

271
00:09:11,957 --> 00:09:14,111
She had come expecting to be hated.

272
00:09:14,111 --> 00:09:16,795
Instead, she was forgiven,
and it undid her.

273
00:09:17,145 --> 00:09:19,615
And then something extraordinary grew out

274
00:09:19,615 --> 00:09:20,578
of the wreckage.

275
00:09:20,578 --> 00:09:21,843
They became friends —

276
00:09:21,843 --> 00:09:23,048
real, close friends.

277
00:09:23,048 --> 00:09:25,517
Together they wrote a book about what had

278
00:09:25,517 --> 00:09:28,228
happened to them both,
called Picking Cotton.

279
00:09:28,228 --> 00:09:30,276
Together, they began to travel the

280
00:09:30,276 --> 00:09:32,565
country, telling their story to police

281
00:09:32,565 --> 00:09:34,432
departments and law schools and

282
00:09:34,432 --> 00:09:36,721
courtrooms, so that the next confident

283
00:09:36,721 --> 00:09:38,768
witness could not convict the next

284
00:09:38,768 --> 00:09:39,551
innocent man.

285
00:09:39,551 --> 00:09:41,298
Because of cases like theirs,

286
00:09:41,298 --> 00:09:43,587
the way police show a witness a lineup

287
00:09:43,587 --> 00:09:44,430
was reformed —

288
00:09:44,430 --> 00:09:46,659
done blind, done carefully, so that a

289
00:09:46,659 --> 00:09:49,128
memory can no longer be nudged toward the

290
00:09:49,128 --> 00:09:49,828
wrong face.

291
00:09:49,828 --> 00:09:52,298
The detective who had once built the case

292
00:09:52,298 --> 00:09:54,768
against Ronald Cotton became one of those

293
00:09:54,768 --> 00:09:57,117
reformers himself, changing how his own

294
00:09:57,117 --> 00:09:59,586
officers ran a lineup so the same mistake

295
00:09:59,586 --> 00:10:00,971
could not happen again.

296
00:10:00,971 --> 00:10:03,381
And years later the survivor and the man

297
00:10:03,381 --> 00:10:05,549
she had wrongly accused were honored

298
00:10:05,549 --> 00:10:06,995
together, side by side —

299
00:10:06,995 --> 00:10:09,043
two people with every reason to be

300
00:10:09,043 --> 00:10:11,294
enemies, standing instead as partners.

301
00:10:11,644 --> 00:10:13,606
And there is one last detail,

302
00:10:13,606 --> 00:10:15,635
almost too perfect to be true.

303
00:10:15,635 --> 00:10:18,409
After he was released, Ronald Cotton took

304
00:10:18,409 --> 00:10:19,109
a job —

305
00:10:19,109 --> 00:10:19,853
at LabCorp.

306
00:10:19,853 --> 00:10:22,694
The very laboratory whose test had finally

307
00:10:22,694 --> 00:10:23,394
set him free.

308
00:10:23,697 --> 00:10:25,593
So go back to the beginning —

309
00:10:25,593 --> 00:10:28,078
to a woman looking her attacker in the

310
00:10:28,078 --> 00:10:30,105
eyes and being absolutely sure.

311
00:10:30,105 --> 00:10:31,478
She was not careless.

312
00:10:31,478 --> 00:10:32,655
She was not lying.

313
00:10:32,655 --> 00:10:35,009
She did everything a good witness is

314
00:10:35,009 --> 00:10:36,055
supposed to do —

315
00:10:36,055 --> 00:10:38,475
and she still sent an innocent man to

316
00:10:38,475 --> 00:10:40,044
prison for eleven years.

317
00:10:40,394 --> 00:10:43,118
That is the part that
should stay with you.

318
00:10:43,118 --> 00:10:45,334
The problem was never that Jennifer

319
00:10:45,334 --> 00:10:47,298
Thompson wasn't certain enough.

320
00:10:47,298 --> 00:10:49,768
The problem is that certainty and truth

321
00:10:49,768 --> 00:10:51,288
are not the same thing —

322
00:10:51,288 --> 00:10:52,808
and a courtroom, a jury,

323
00:10:52,808 --> 00:10:54,835
all of us, we tend to treat them

324
00:10:54,835 --> 00:10:55,785
as if they are.

325
00:10:55,785 --> 00:10:58,129
Jennifer Thompson has spent years now

326
00:10:58,129 --> 00:11:00,282
saying it out loud, so that no one

327
00:11:00,282 --> 00:11:02,436
can miss it: memory can be honest,

328
00:11:02,436 --> 00:11:05,286
and confident, and still
be completely wrong.

329
00:11:05,636 --> 00:11:08,062
An innocent man lost eleven years to a

330
00:11:08,062 --> 00:11:10,871
memory that was honestly,
confidently wrong.

331
00:11:10,871 --> 00:11:13,488
And instead of spending the rest of their

332
00:11:13,488 --> 00:11:15,850
lives as the accuser and the accused,

333
00:11:15,850 --> 00:11:18,531
two people decided to spend it making sure

334
00:11:18,531 --> 00:11:20,255
it happens to fewer others.

335
00:11:20,255 --> 00:11:22,042
If you have ever been sure —

336
00:11:22,042 --> 00:11:23,319
really, truly sure —

337
00:11:23,319 --> 00:11:25,043
and turned out to be wrong,

338
00:11:25,043 --> 00:11:27,213
hit like, so that a jury somewhere

339
00:11:27,213 --> 00:11:29,320
remembers that it's possible too.
